          %p We have that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The commutativity of the diagram on the left says that if one forms a product measure and then takes its marginals, one recovers the two factors. In other words,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em every product probability measure is necessarily the product of its marginals
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                preserves the terminal object, i.e.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(1)\cong 1
                (see at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+monad"} affine monad
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
                and for most
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
            %li
              %p
                In general, the diagram on the right does not commute: if one starts with a joint distribution and then forms its marginals, the original distribution may be different from the product of the marginals. Therefore marginalization is a destructive operation, forgetting possible
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+interaction"} stochastic interaction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                preserves
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
                and of other
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
                can be explained, to a large degree, by their failure to preserve products. (More on this below, and at
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+category#eqdet"} Markov category
          %h3#in_the_category_of_markov_kernels In the category of Markov kernels
          %p As we have seen above, a joint distribution, in general, encodes more information than the pair of its marginals, and cannot always be recovered by forming the product distribution.
          %p
            We can express this equivalently by looking at the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Markov+kernels"} Markov kernels
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+categories"} Kleisli categories
            of similar
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+monads"} probability monads
          %p
            First of all, recall that the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            inherits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal structure
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Meas"} Meas
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+spaces"} measurable spaces
          %p
            This product in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stoch"} Stoch
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            requires that given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
